# Security (all changes)

Full detail: `docs/security.md`.

## Secrets & config

- Never commit `.env`, credentials, real employee data, AWS account IDs, or ARNs
- **Do not edit** `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`, or service-specific env files (`backend/.env`, `expiry-job/.env`, `frontend/.env`) — the user owns those. **May edit** `.env.example` files (root and per-service templates) when adding or documenting new config keys.
- No hardcoded secrets or fallback JWT secrets; read from env
- AWS resource names via env only (`EVENTBRIDGE_BUS_NAME`, `SQS_QUEUE_NAME`, etc.)

## API & auth

- All routes except `POST /auth/login` require `JwtAuthGuard`
- Passport: `passport-local` (login), `passport-jwt` (protected routes)
- Passwords: bcrypt only; never log or return `passwordHash`
- Do not expose hard delete via API; soft-delete/archive only

## Input & data access

- Validate all bodies with DTOs + `class-validator` before service/DB layer
- TypeORM parameterized queries only; never interpolate user input into SQL
- Clients cannot set `status` directly — server recalculates from dates
- Integer IDs are guessable: do not rely on obscurity; validate resource access on `:id` routes

## Expiry job

- Service account via env; login once per run; no permanent API tokens
- `bulk-status` accepts only `active` | `expiring` | `expired`
- Production: HTTPS only for API calls

## Transport

- CORS: explicit allowlist from env, not `*` in production

When planning features, flag auth, validation, and audit-trail impact before coding.
